
  Next question: [1mwhat do the other forms ask this thing for?[0m

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether we're building a [3msession[0m (something that knows whether anyone is logged in, who, and how to forget
  them) or just a [3mstored identifier[0m (a value other code reads to stamp onto what it sends). Same storage underneath,
  very different interface — and the interface is what your later forms get locked into.

  - [1mSession module[0m — owns current-user state: [38;5;153mgetCurrentUser()[39m, set on login, [38;5;153mclear()[39m on logout, persisted. Buys one
    authoritative answer to "who's logged in?" and a real logged-out state. Costs a logout path and forcing every
    consumer to handle "nobody." Going from this to the simpler one later is free — consumers just ignore the extra
    methods.
  - [1mStored identifier only[0m — a persisted [38;5;153muserId[39m string other code reads when it needs to tag a submission or a log line.
    Buys a much smaller surface, today. Costs: no logout, no answer to "is anyone logged in," and each consumer invents
    its own handling of the missing case. Going from this to a session later means touching every consumer — this is
    the direction that's expensive.

  [1mMy recommendation: the session module.[0m You said other forms will need it and that it has to persist. The moment a
  second form reads a persisted user ID, its very next question is "and what if there isn't one / what if they logged
  out?" A bare string makes every caller answer that separately and inconsistently. Since the cheap direction is session
  → simpler and the costly direction is the reverse, the session shape is the lower-risk default even if you only ever
  use [38;5;153mgetCurrentUser()[39m.
